SUMMARY {"label": "P0C-llamacpp-r1", "load_s": 34.3, "vram_idle_mib": 14207, "vram_peak_mib": 14679, "rows": [{"depth": 1000, "prompt_n": 1000, "prefill_tps": 477.6, "decode_tps": 39.4, "decode_ms": 25.38, "wall_s": 11.8, "text": " How to Curse directed by Josie Rourke . How to Curse was performed at the Bush Theatre in the London Borough of Hammersmith and Fulham . In a review for The Daily Telegraph , critic Charles Spencer described Boulter 's performance as , \" a fine performance as the decent young man trapped in a web of lies . \" \n Boulter starred in two films in 2008 , Daylight Robbery by filmmaker Paris Leonti , and Donkey Punch directed by Olly Blackburn . In May 2008 , Boulter made a guest appearance on a two @-@ part episode arc of the television series Waking the Dead , titled \" Innocence and Experience \" . He appeared on the television series Survivors in November 2008 , as \" Matt \" . He had a recurring role in ten episodes of the television series Casualty in 2010 , as \" Kieron Fletcher \" . Boulter starred in the 2011 film Mercenaries directed by Paris Leonti . \n \n\n = = Personal life = = \n \n Boulter lives in London . \n \n\n = = Filmography = = \n \n \n = = = Film = = = \n \n < table class=\"wikitable\" > \n < table > \n \n = = = Television = = = \n \n < table class=\"wikitable\" > \n < table > \n \n = = = Theatre = = = \n \n < table class=\"wikitable\" > \n < table > \n \n = = Awards and nominations = = \n \n < table class=\"wikitable\" > \n < table > \n \n\n = = References = = \n \n \n = = External links = = \n \n Robert Boulter at the Internet Movie Database \n Robert Boulter at the Official Site for the Royal Court Theatre \n Robert Boulter at the National Theatre \n \n \n \n\n = Robert Browning = \n \n Robert Browning ( 14 June 1812"}, {"depth": 32000, "prompt_n": 31004, "prefill_tps": 648.8, "decode_tps": 42.2, "decode_ms": 23.7, "wall_s": 57.0, "text": " who escaped , some were picked up by the 25th Division . \n Meanwhile , the North Korean attack against the 9th Infantry Regiment 's B Company on Hill 209 had driven the company from its position . The 9th Infantry Regiment 's reserve company , E Company , was sent to retake the hill . It was unable to reach its objective . The North Koreans had already reached Cloverleaf Hill and Obong @-@ ni Ridge . \n The North Koreans also attacked the 2nd Battalion , 9th Infantry , north of Yongsan . The 2nd Battalion had been in division reserve and had been committed to the front during the night . It had dug in on the low hills west of Yongsan in the early morning hours . At 07 : 00 a heavy mortar barrage struck the battalion 's positions and a tank was knocked out . At 08 : 00 North Korean infantry attacked the battalion . The attack was repelled , but the battalion was pinned down and could not move to reinforce the 9th Infantry . \n At 10 : 00 , General Bradley , commanding Task Force Bradley , sent a patrol of four M26 Pershing tanks under Captain Robert L. Anderson to reconnoiter the road from Yongsan to the Cloverleaf Hill @-@ Obong @-@ ni Ridge pass . Anderson 's patrol was to proceed as far as possible to the pass and then return . The patrol reached the pass and found the area empty . Anderson then drove 1 @.@ 5 miles ( 2 @.@ 4 km ) beyond the pass and was fired on by a North Korean machine gun . He returned to Yongsan . \n At 11 : 00 , a company @-@ sized North Korean force attacked Yongsan . The town was defended by a small"}, {"depth": 131000, "prompt_n": 99004, "prefill_tps": 543.3, "decode_tps": 38.86, "decode_ms": 25.73, "wall_s": 192.1, "text": " the French soldiers who died in the battle . \n In 1905 , the 100th anniversary of the battle was celebrated in the Wachau Valley . The celebrations included a re @-@ enactment of the battle and a parade . In 1905 , the Krems @-@ Stein bridge was named the D\u00fcrenstein Bridge . \n In 1913 , the Austrian @-@ Hungarian army named a mountain gun after the battle . \n In 1930 , the Austrian army named a regiment after the battle . \n In 1934 , the Austrian army named a mountain gun after the battle . \n In 1937 , the Austrian army named a mountain gun after the battle . \n In 1943 , the German army named a mountain gun after the battle . \n In 1955 , the Austrian army named a mountain gun after the battle . \n In 1960 , the Austrian army named a mountain gun after the battle . \n In 1961 , the Austrian army named a mountain gun after the battle . \n In 1962 , the Austrian army named a mountain gun after the battle . \n In 1963 , the Austrian army named a mountain gun after the battle . \n In 1964 , the Austrian army named a mountain gun after the battle . \n In 1965 , the Austrian army named a mountain gun after the battle . \n In 1966 , the Austrian army named a mountain gun after the battle . \n In 1967 , the Austrian army named a mountain gun after the battle . \n In 1968 , the Austrian army named a mountain gun after the battle . \n In 1969 , the Austrian army named a mountain gun after the battle . \n In "}, {"depth": 245000, "prompt_n": 114004, "prefill_tps": 375.9, "decode_tps": 32.78, "decode_ms": 30.51, "wall_s": 315.0, "text": " 2012 United States presidential election campaign by depicting a view of the world from 9th Avenue as seen by Mitt Romney . The cover depicted the world as seen by Romney as a series of large blocks of money . \n The 2013 cover of the New York Times Magazine depicted a view of the world from 9th Avenue as seen by Edward Snowden . The cover depicted the world as seen by Snowden as a series of large blocks of data . \n \n = = Legacy = = \n \n View of the World from 9th Avenue is regarded as one of the greatest magazine covers of recent generations . It is studied by art students around the world . The work has been exhibited in the United States and abroad . The University of Pennsylvania 's The New Yorker : Fifty Years of Illustration exhibition , which ran from November 1995 to February 1996 , exhibited the work along with alternate versions and sketches . The work was exhibited in the United States in 2004 at the New York Public Library 's \" New York : The New Yorker sees the city , 1925 \u2013 2004 \" exhibit . The work was exhibited in 2007 at the New York Historical Society 's \" New York : The New Yorker sees the city , 1925 \u2013 2004 \" exhibit . The work was exhibited in 2008 at the New York Historical Society 's \" New York : The New Yorker sees the city , 1925 \u2013 2004 \" exhibit . \n \n \n = 2011\u201312 FC Barcelona season = \n \n The 2011 \u2013 12 FC Barcelona season was Barcelona 's 113th season in existence and the club 's 41st consecutive season in the top flight of Spanish football . Barcelona"}], "log": "$BENCH/p0c-20260927/P0C-llamacpp-r1-20260927-151850.log"}
